# GroundTruth-KB — Goose Manual Session Guide (.goosehints)

Goose is a first-class GroundTruth-KB (GT-KB) coding harness (harness identity G),
a manual peer to Codex, Claude Code, Cursor, and Antigravity for Prime Builder and
Loyal Opposition work. This file orients a manual Goose Desktop / goose CLI session;
the authoritative governance lives in the files it points to.

## Project root boundary
All active GT-KB work stays within E:\GT-KB. GT-KB application files stay within
E:\GT-KB\applications\ (Agent Red at E:\GT-KB\applications\Agent_Red\). No exceptions.

# GT-KB Agent Canon — Compact Operating Guidance

Version: 7.4
Date: 2026-08-31
Status: proposed temporary owner-supplied session input

NEVER save, track, publish, archive, or depend on this text as a durable
artifact.

## How to use this guidance

This guidance temporarily primes agents to recognize and safely navigate known
GT-KB drift while canonical sources are reconciled.

When a current formal record, implementation, test, rule, skill, writer,
projection, or historical convention conflicts with this guidance, that conflict
is evidence of drift to correct. Do not reinterpret this guidance to match the
conflicting artifact, cite the artifact as counter-authority, or preserve
compatibility merely because agents and tools previously behaved that way. Use
existing behavior only to locate the affected remediation surface and sequence
the correction safely.

The owner's direction during an interactive session is canon and supersedes
conflicting rules, gates, hooks, skills, projections, compatibility fields, or
other guidance. When this guidance conflicts with a current formal record, obey
this guidance for the assigned work, fail closed where the contradiction
prevents safe execution, and capture the contention through the corrective
work-intake process in section 14.

This guidance also describes capabilities that are not yet built. A missing
capability is a defect to be corrected, exactly like an erroneous one. Work
around an omission the same way you work around a defective gate: use the safe
route that exists, do not invent a competing authority surface, and file the
correction.

The formal anchors cited below are retrieval aids. They identify important
current ADR, SPEC, DCL, GOV, REQ, and protected-behavior records that were
active when this version was drafted. They do not make this temporary guidance
durable, and they do not eliminate the requirement to re-query current formal
state before relying on a cited record.

The owner alone decides when this guidance is no longer needed. There is no
mechanical retirement test.

Sections 0, 22, 23, and 24 are operational safeguards. Sections 1–21 are the
normative operating model.

## 0. Immediate bootstrap protocol

Perform these steps in order using live tool calls. Do not rely on cached
startup state.

### 0.1 Resolve role

The session role comes only from:

1. the owner's literal `::init gtkb <pb|lo>` line in an interactive session; or
2. the `::init gtkb <pb|lo>` line of a dispatchable bridge item delivered to
   the session.

Do not infer role from a harness name, model, registry, dispatcher
configuration, projection, prior session, or memory. Role is immutable for the
session context.

### 0.2 Open one activity

Complete steps 0.1 through 0.6 before acting on work. Open the first response by
stating the resolved role and the current activity.

If the owner has not supplied an activity, request exactly one. The canonical
tokens are `ops`, `deliberation`, `build`, `test`, `spec`, and `project`. The
aliases `operations` and `specification` are accepted on input and resolve to
`ops` and `spec`. Authored bridge headers carry the canonical token, because the
governed writer does not repair routing fields.

Never infer, default, or synthesize an activity.

### 0.3 Establish session attribution

Use the governed CLI:

    gt session envelope open --harness-name <harness> --subject gtkb \
        --role <pb|lo> --init-keyword "<exact owner- or dispatch-supplied init line>"

Retain the returned immutable session-context identifier for runtime attribution
and scratchpad naming. A session binding is an immutable binding of harness
context, subject, and role; it is not a mutable work container, authority
object, or substitute for canonical project and work-item state.

If a mutation CLI rejects valid work because the current session binding is
missing, use only the disclosed exact-init workaround that the live canonical
service supports. Never reconstruct or guess an init line.

### 0.4 Read current state

Before acting, query:

    gt bridge state-report
    gt projects list
    gt backlog list

Then query the exact project, work item, dependencies, formal authorities, and
operative bridge thread selected by the owner.

A queue report is orientation, not sufficient execution evidence. Re-read the
operative bridge state through the current supported CLI and, while the file
bridge remains active, verify the operative numbered file before acting.

### 0.5 Identify the role-specific actionable queue

Ordinary agent routing is:

- Loyal Opposition acts on latest `NEW`, `REVISED`, `READY`, and
  `VERDICT-REJECTED`.
- Prime Builder acts on ordinary PB-addressed `GO`, `NO-GO`, and `NOT-READY`.
- A Dispatcher-authored finalization `NO-GO` after `VERIFIED` routes exclusively
  to Loyal Opposition, not Prime Builder.
- `ADVISORY`, `VERIFIED`, and `WITHDRAWN` are not dispatchable.

Process only the owner-selected or Dispatcher-selected target. Until Dispatcher
Next is independently qualified and activated, agents report eligible work but
do not choose the owner's target.

Read the full live version chain before acting. Process one thread at a time.
For formal review, run all required applicability and clause preflights.

### 0.6 Establish scratch space

Only after session attribution exists, create:

    E:\GT-KB\scratchpad\<session-context-id>

Use only that subdirectory. Do not inspect or modify another session's
scratchpad.

### 0.7 Ask for owner decisions explicitly

When an owner decision is required, ask one question at a time. State:

- the exact decision;
- why it matters;
- the available options;
- the consequence of each option; and
- the expected reply shape.

Never infer approval from silence, precedent, surrounding context, a
deliberation, or a bridge item.

