## 1. Semantic closure and activation

### Semantic closure

A broad architectural change is complete only when its smallest provable
affected semantic closure is reconciled and activated.

That closure includes, where applicable:

- active SPEC, ADR, DCL, GOV, REQ, and protected-behavior records;
- glossary terms, component names, and artifact conventions;
- project topology, dependencies, authorization state, and work-item scope;
- source, schemas, migrations, service contracts, and CLI surfaces;
- tests and executable assertions;
- rules, skills, hooks, generators, projectors, and generated projections;
- System Architecture Document shards and structured intersections; and
- removal or supersession of contradictory current material.

Search by concept, behavior, routing, and state transition, not merely known
identifiers or exact vocabulary.

Use one shared change bundle with separately stated results for every affected
project. Present the owner with:

- a readable before/after brief;
- exact formal postimages;
- exact project and authorization postimages;
- affected tests and commands;
- removal and supersession effects;
- activation and rollback boundaries; and
- explicit non-materiality evidence for excluded scope.

Independent Loyal Opposition verifies every affected project result.

Reconcile, approve, and freeze the reviewed snapshot before activation. Material
drift invalidates only the smallest provably affected scope. Unsupported claims
of non-materiality fail closed.

### Activation

Activation means the work product is live in GT-KB and in use: working and
available in the development environment itself, where the code may be executed
to run test workloads, review, and ordinary work.

Activation is the direct and automatic consequence of the project commit defined
in section 7. Nobody decides it. No agent performs it as a separate act. The
canonical state transition that records activation follows from the commit, and
that record is a mirror of the Git fact, never a second source for it.

Activation attaches to the authored work product. Materializing a derivation —
running a projector, installing a CLI, regenerating a dashboard or a System
Architecture Document shard — is an operational task and never gates activation.
The test is whether the derivation can be produced from the committed work
product, not whether it has been produced.

Activation is not a transaction and does not roll back. Git provides history;
GT-KB does not model activation reversal. Deactivation is the forward work that
changes the subject so that prior work no longer takes effect.

Activation is a distinct concept from authorization, terminality, and
verification, though the project commit triggers activation and terminality
together. Authorization is the owner's approval to implement, recorded before
work proceeds; activation is a mechanical consequence recorded after the commit.

Formal anchors:

- `ADR-SPEC-COVERAGE-ARCHITECTURE-001` v2
- `DCL-SPEC-RELEVANCE-CLOSURE-001` v2
- `DCL-IMPLEMENTATION-PROPOSAL-SPEC-LINKAGE-MANDATORY-001` v2
- `DCL-VERIFIED-SPEC-DERIVED-TESTING-MANDATORY-001` v2
- `DCL-SUPERSEDED-SOT-LEAKAGE-001` v1
- `GOV-CROSS-CUTTING-REQUIREMENTS-MECHANICAL-ENFORCEMENT-001` v2

## 2. System Architecture Document

The System Architecture Document is the designated generated architecture
reference. It is Git-versioned, clone-reconstructible work product with no
independent authority. Every material claim must resolve to current formal
sources.

The SAD contains:

- one shared GT-KB baseline;
- one project-specific delta or deterministic `no_delta` result;
- component, capability, and source-of-truth maps;
- subsystem responsibilities and boundaries;
- CLI and service integration surfaces;
- artifact names, formats, and conventions;
- role and activity responsibilities;
- architecture rules, defaults, biases, qualities, and fitness functions;
- required tests; and
- cross-project intersections.

The SAD is generated from typed canonical sources and a structured intersection
graph. Agents normally load only the relevant shards. Generating a shard is an
operational task under section 1 and does not gate activation.

When role, activity, project, work item, and target scope are known, context
loading composes:

- the shared baseline;
- the exact project delta;
- relevant graph-native shards;
- applicable formal references;
- project intersections; and
- the smallest provable affected closure.

Proposal, authorization, implementation start, protected effect, report, and
verification cannot rely on an incomplete required closure.

Every proposal and report identifies the relevant SAD revision, formal corpus
snapshot, project delta, and closure evidence when those services exist and are
active.

Formal-closure notice: no active formal artifact explicitly naming the complete
SAD contract or the WordPress Style and Norms Reference was found during the
live 2026-08-30 citation pass. Until those carriers exist and are active, this
section remains owner-supplied canon and the missing formal closure is a
platform defect, not permission to invent a competing SAD mechanism.

Related formal anchors:

- `ADR-SPEC-COVERAGE-ARCHITECTURE-001` v2
- `DCL-CROSS-CUTTING-REQUIREMENTS-REGISTRY-001` v2
- `DCL-CANONICAL-CARRIER-NONAUTHORITY-001` v2
- `PB-GTKB-GET-HEALTHY-BASELINE-001` v2

## 3. Authority, programs, projects, and work items

A program is a plan. It sequences projects and grants no execution authority.

### Authorization

Authorization is a field on the project row. It holds `authorized` or
`not authorized`. The owner's direction sets it; nothing else does.
Authorization is the action, directed by the owner, that sets that value. There
is no authorization artifact, record, envelope, packet, receipt, token, or
identifier, and none is created or referenced. New projects are created
`authorized`.

Authorization is a hard gate on dispatch. Its purpose is work ordering: it says
this project's work may proceed now. That is its whole meaning. It is not a
permission system, a security boundary, or a trust check.

Authorization has no scope, expiry, mutation-class, or forbidden-operation
semantics. Work-item scope is project membership. Change scope is the
implementation proposal's target paths together with the applicable formal
authority.

Legacy work-item `approval_state` and every PAUTH-shaped record, field, packet,
receipt, or evidence token grants and denies nothing. The
`project_authorizations` table and its consumers are retired, including its
scope, expiry, mutation-class, and forbidden-operation semantics.

Projects are authorized by default unless the owner has a concrete reason its
work should not proceed yet, principally dependency or work-order sequencing, or
insufficient specificity to implement. Unauthorized means not yet, not
forbidden.

### Dispatch

A work item belongs to one or more projects. It is dispatchable when any project
it belongs to is `authorized`.

The dispatcher — the owner, and Dispatcher Next once activated — does not
dispatch a work item whose projects are all `not authorized`. A dispatched work
item may proceed, and the fact of dispatch is the only proof any agent requires.
Dispatch is either automated or manual: owner direction to work on a work item
is a dispatch and is itself complete proof. A dispatched agent does not
re-derive or re-verify whether the work was authorized, and work is never
refused on the ground that it is not authorized.

Owner direction to implement a work item bears implicitly on the project the
work item is in: either that project becomes `authorized`, or the work item
moves to a project that already is. In interactive sessions this is decided
case-by-case.

Until Dispatcher Next is independently qualified and activated, all dispatch is
manual.

### Membership

Owner-directed addition, removal, or movement of a work item preserves each
affected project's existing authorization value unless the owner explicitly
changes it. Record the resulting current state and append-only transition
history, then read it back.

Agents cannot set authorization. An agent-initiated membership change leaves
every affected project's value unchanged, and may leave the work item
undispatchable until the owner acts.

Terminal work-item membership is immutable.

All work items belong to projects. An unattached work item is an error to
correct through owner-governed reconciliation, not an invitation to create an ad
hoc project.

### Readiness

Authorization is not the whole of readiness. Dispatched work still requires:

- a specification-linked implementation proposal;
- independent Loyal Opposition GO before implementation;
- a linked executable test for every new implementation work item;
- a matching live work-intent claim; and
- the applicable active formal authority for what it changes.

These are review, evidence, and concurrency gates. They are not authorization,
and passing them does not make them authorization.

Use one bridge chain per work item. Work items verify independently. The project
commits once, when all of its work items are `VERIFIED`, per section 7.

Specifications, ADRs, DCLs, GOV records, and other recognized formal records
carry authority through their current active state. Deliberations do not
authorize or prove approval.

An ADVISORY is non-authoritative. When the owner explicitly assigns work from an
ADVISORY, that assignment is the dispatch and the authority for the assigned
scope.

Authority: `GOV-PROJECT-IMPLEMENTATION-AUTHORIZATION-001`,
`GOV-PROJECT-REQUIRES-LINKED-SPECIFICATIONS-001`,
`GOV-ARTIFACT-AUTHORITY-HIERARCHY-001`,
`DCL-CANONICAL-CARRIER-NONAUTHORITY-001`, `GOV-STANDING-BACKLOG-001`.

## 4. Roles, contexts, activities, and provenance

Roles belong to session contexts, not harnesses, vendors, processes,
workstations, models, or dispatcher registry entries.

The exact `::init gtkb <pb|lo>` supplied by the owner or a dispatchable bridge
header establishes the receiving context's role. It is immutable for that
context. Role switching is prohibited.

Prime Builder:

- investigates;
- proposes;
- performs authorized implementation;
- performs authorized operations only in the `ops` activity;
- may author `NEW`, `REVISED`, `READY`, `VERDICT-REJECTED`, pre-GO `WITHDRAWN`,
  and `ADVISORY`;
- never authors `GO`, ordinary `NO-GO`, `NOT-READY`, or `VERIFIED`;
- never formally reviews or commits its own context's work.

Loyal Opposition:

- independently reviews and tests;
- authors `GO`, ordinary `NO-GO`, `NOT-READY`, `VERIFIED`, and `ADVISORY`;
- commits the independently reviewed work product;
- never formally reviews its own context's work;
- never performs an operational mutation it will verify.

Same-role internal subagents are allowed only for bounded read-only research and
analysis. They receive no mutation, lifecycle, bridge-authoring, authorization,
or formal-verdict power. The controlling agent independently verifies their
findings.

There is exactly one current transient activity: `ops`, `deliberation`, `build`,
`test`, `spec`, or `project`.

`::open` selects activity but grants no role or authority. Activity remains
current until another explicit `::open`, `::close`, `::wrap`, or context
termination.

The session binding is an immutable attribution record. It is not a mutable
session-state object, work envelope, approval hierarchy, or authority store.

Governed acts carry sufficient authored provenance for attribution, routing, and
independent-review checks. This is cooperative governance, not a claim of
security isolation against an agent with owner-level filesystem access.

Formal anchors:

