- `ADR-SESSION-MARKER-AND-ACTIVITY-RECORD-MODEL-001` v3
- `DCL-INIT-BOUND-SESSION-IDENTITY-001` v2
- `DCL-ACTIVITY-CONTEXT-MANIFEST-001` v4
- `GOV-COOPERATIVE-GOVERNANCE-AND-AUDITABILITY-001` v2

## 5. Close, wrap, and handoff

`::close` performs bounded activity-scoped governed harvest.

`::wrap` performs context-wide governed harvest and may display a
self-contained, owner-copyable continuation prompt.

Neither operation automatically commits, pushes, deploys, authorizes, or
activates work.

Durable knowledge is written only through canonical governed writers.

A handoff is ephemeral owner-copyable text, never an on-disk or database
authority object. It may contain narrative, identifiers, canonical pointers,
ordered steps, commands, hazards, and timestamped observations. No fact required
for continuation may exist only in the handoff. The receiving context re-queries
canonical state.

Authority: `ADR-SESSION-MARKER-AND-ACTIVITY-RECORD-MODEL-001`,
`DCL-ACTIVITY-CONTEXT-MANIFEST-001`, `DCL-INIT-BOUND-SESSION-IDENTITY-001`,
`DCL-SESSION-WRAP-UP-AUTOMATION-SAFETY-001`,
`PB-SESSION-WRAP-UP-PROACTIVE-001`, `DCL-CANONICAL-CARRIER-NONAUTHORITY-001`.

## 6. Bridge protocol

Every agent-authored dispatchable item begins with these three lines, in any
order:

    ::init gtkb <pb|lo>
    ::open <activity>
    <status-token>

Typed metadata follows them. The status token must appear within the first three
nonblank lines; the governed writer rejects any head that does not carry exactly
one status token there.

The author supplies the complete header. The governed writer validates and
atomically records it but never synthesizes, reorders, repairs, or fills routing
fields. No metadata key may appear twice. The envelope names the next responder,
not the author: an LO-authored GO addressed to Prime Builder carries the `pb`
marker, and the default envelope activity for a status-bearing artifact is
`build`.

### Required metadata

Every dispatchable item carries:

    bridge_kind
    Document, Version, Date
    author_identity, author_harness_id, author_session_context_id, author_model
    recipient_role

`recipient_role` names the next responder and is the sole routing key.
`target_role` is a retired duplicate and is not written.

Every implementation proposal additionally carries:

    Project, Work Item, target_paths, test_artifact_targets

### Status vocabulary — exactly ten

- NEW — Addressee Loyal Opposition; authored by Prime Builder; new
  implementation proposals only.
- REVISED — Addressee Loyal Opposition; authored by Prime Builder; revised
  implementation proposals only.
- READY — Addressee Loyal Opposition; authored by Prime Builder; valid only as
  an implementation report after GO; must carry
  `bridge_kind: implementation_report`, which the governed writer validates.
  READY cannot begin a thread. It replaces the historical use of NEW for
  post-implementation reports.
- VERDICT-REJECTED — Addressee Loyal Opposition; authored by Prime Builder; used
  only to reject a governance-noncompliant, PB-actionable Loyal Opposition
  verdict and route a fresh Loyal Opposition correction.
- GO — Addressee Prime Builder; authored by Loyal Opposition; approves a
  proposal for implementation.
- NO-GO — Addressee Prime Builder; authored by Loyal Opposition; rejects a
  proposal and requires revision. Dispatcher may also author the
  finalization-repair NO-GO defined in section 7, which is addressed exclusively
  to Loyal Opposition.
- NOT-READY — Addressee Prime Builder; authored by Loyal Opposition; rejects an
  implementation report and requires a corrected report. It is the report-phase
  counterpart of NO-GO, and exists so that no token is lawful in both the
  proposal phase and the report phase.
- VERIFIED — authored by Loyal Opposition only; records review completion;
  terminal for agents and not dispatchable; only Dispatcher may follow it, per
  section 7.
- WITHDRAWN — authored by Prime Builder only; valid only before GO; terminal and
  not dispatchable.
- ADVISORY — authored by either role at any time; not dispatchable.

This vocabulary is canon now. Where the governed writer does not yet accept a
status, the writer is defective. File the correction under section 14 before
proceeding, and state the substitution explicitly in the bridge item body. Never
substitute a superseded status silently because the old one still works — that
is exactly how the defect propagates.

### Legal transition table — complete

    NEW                              -> GO | NO-GO | WITHDRAWN
    REVISED                          -> GO | NO-GO | WITHDRAWN
    NO-GO (proposal)                 -> REVISED | WITHDRAWN | VERDICT-REJECTED
    GO                               -> READY | VERDICT-REJECTED
    READY                            -> VERIFIED | NOT-READY
    NOT-READY                        -> READY | VERDICT-REJECTED
    VERDICT-REJECTED                 -> GO | NO-GO | NOT-READY
    VERIFIED                         -> NO-GO (Dispatcher-authored only; section 7)
    NO-GO (Dispatcher finalization)  -> VERIFIED
    ADVISORY                         -> ADVISORY

WITHDRAWN is terminal and has no successors. VERIFIED is terminal for agents:
only Dispatcher may follow it, and only with a typed system NO-GO when the
project commit it belongs to fails, per section 7. That Dispatcher NO-GO routes
a fresh Loyal Opposition finalization action rather than Prime Builder work, and
the verified work bytes remain frozen.

A later ADVISORY on the same advisory thread is the latest advisory message.
ADVISORY is not part of an implementation lifecycle, and no other status may
follow it on that thread. Work derived from an ADVISORY begins a fresh NEW chain
that cites the advisory.

VERDICT-REJECTED may immediately follow only an agent-authored, PB-addressed
Loyal Opposition `GO`, `NO-GO`, or `NOT-READY`. It may never follow VERIFIED,
WITHDRAWN, ADVISORY, or a Dispatcher-authored finalization NO-GO: those are
non-dispatchable or system-authored, and no protocol entity receives or rejects
them.

NO-GO has two forms, distinguished by author, not by the artifact adjudicated. A
Loyal-Opposition-authored NO-GO rejects a proposal and routes to Prime Builder
for REVISED. A Dispatcher-authored NO-GO reports a failed project commit and
routes to Loyal Opposition for finalization repair. Report rejection is never
NO-GO; it is NOT-READY. A validator determines the form from the author and the
predecessor status, and rejects `NO-GO -> READY` as invalid.

### Core workflow

NEW proposal -> GO verdict -> Prime Builder implementation -> READY report ->
Loyal Opposition verification -> VERIFIED verdict. When every work item in the
project is VERIFIED, the project commits once, per section 7.

No implementation lands without Loyal Opposition review. Prime Builder receives
and acts on ordinary PB-addressed GO, NO-GO, and NOT-READY. Loyal Opposition
receives and acts on NEW, REVISED, READY, VERDICT-REJECTED, and the
Dispatcher-authored finalization NO-GO. ADVISORY, VERIFIED, and WITHDRAWN are
not dispatchable.

### Obsolete statuses

NO-ACTION and DEFERRED are invalid, obsolete bridge statuses. New writes reject
both. Historical files bearing them may be displayed only as inert historical
text; they confer no routing, lifecycle, claim, lease, or implementation state,
are never rewritten or normalized, and no alias or crosswalk exists. Every
nonterminal chain whose operative status is NO-ACTION is resolved, marked
abandoned, or reauthored from current canonical facts.

### Other retained rules

A malformed dispatchable header makes the item non-dispatchable and quarantines
bridge output from that context. Only a fresh same-role context may reconstruct
the work from canonical facts and reauthor, adopt, or discard each item. Parser
success alone does not restore trust.

Bridge material is ephemeral coordination and never authority. It must not be
committed, cited by authoritative artifacts, or required for canonical recovery.
Do not place references to non-canonical sources, including scratchpad paths, in
bridge items.

WITHDRAWN aborts an entire pre-GO attempt: the bridge chain must not be
committed, disposable chain material is deleted, and the work item returns to
its pre-proposal queue state unless subsequently deferred through the CLI. Prime
Builder alone may withdraw, only while holding the current lifecycle action, and
only before GO. Loyal Opposition never withdraws; if it judges a proposal dead
it issues NO-GO. Once GO authorizes implementation, the thread runs to VERIFIED
or is marked abandoned in canonical attempt state. Committed work cannot be
withdrawn.

Chain repair has two remedies, and the unbroken prefix decides which applies.
When an unbroken consecutive chain reaches a lawful current status, continue from
its last consecutive item; conflicting subsequently-numbered items are discarded
and replaced with fresh items that let the unbroken chain reach terminal status.
When no unbroken prefix reaches a lawful current status — the chain is lost,
corrupt, or irreparable — mark it abandoned in canonical attempt state and start
a fresh NEW chain from current canonical facts. In both cases never inherit GO,
claim, lease, or implementation authority from discarded or abandoned material,
and never fabricate a WITHDRAWN message for an item no agent can truthfully
author.

Authority: `GOV-FILE-BRIDGE-AUTHORITY-001`,
`ADR-BRIDGE-ARTIFACT-HEAD-ENVELOPE-001`,
`DCL-BRIDGE-ENVELOPE-LINE-AUTHORING-PLACEMENT-001`,
`DCL-BRIDGE-KIND-TAXONOMY-ENUM-001`, `SPEC-BRIDGE-STATUS-PHASE-DISTINCT-001`,
`SPEC-CANONICAL-INIT-KEYWORD-SYNTAX-001`, `DCL-BRIDGE-CLAIM-LIFECYCLE-001`,
`DCL-BRIDGE-PROPOSAL-PROJECT-LINKAGE-MANDATORY-001`,
`DCL-BRIDGE-VERDICT-AUQ-CLASS-MARKER-001`.

## 7. Verification, commit, activation, and terminality

Verification is the emission of a verdict on the completion of the work
described in the implementation proposal and referenced by its cited sources.
Loyal Opposition verifies that the work product has been tested against the
proposal's stated intent and emits `VERIFIED` for that work item.

`VERIFIED` records review completion. It does not assert that a commit exists.

A verified work product is held back from activation until every work item in
its project is `VERIFIED`.

Because the project is the activation unit, a project is sized to the work that
completes together. A project holding work that will not complete cannot commit,
and that is a sizing error to correct through owner-governed membership, not a
condition for an agent to work around.

If a work item's verified bytes change before the project commit, that work item
returns to the reviewing role for fresh verification. Only bytes verified in
their committed form are committed.

When the last work item in a project reaches `VERIFIED`, the CLI instructs the
verifying agent to commit that project's work items. That commit:

1. is a single commit covering the project's complete verified work product;
2. cites every work item it retires, in the form `(WI-NNNN)`;
3. excludes all bridge material; and
4. excludes generated projections and other derived output, which the
   operational refresh in section 8 owns.

The commit activates the work product and establishes work-item terminality
together. Publication of that commit to the registered repository provides
durability; it is not a separate terminality condition.

Dispatcher confirms canonical terminal state, prevents redispatch, releases
controls, and removes disposable bridge material. Until Dispatcher Next is
activated the owner performs this role. A Dispatcher-authored bridge item
carries `::init gtkb lo`, routes to Loyal Opposition, and may carry no status
other than the finalization NO-GO below.

If an instructed project commit does not complete, its work items remain
`VERIFIED` and uncommitted. Dispatcher emits a fresh typed system `NO-GO` with
exact reason and evidence, such as `commit_not_confirmed`, addressed to Loyal
Opposition. The reviewed work bytes remain frozen. After correction and
independent confirmation, Loyal Opposition may issue a fresh `VERIFIED` and the
commit proceeds.

A terminal work item cannot be reopened or withdrawn. Reversal or removal of
committed material is new forward work under a new work item.

Do not rewrite ordinary Git history or delete formal history. Supersede or
retire formal records.

At terminal closure, purge bridge payload content. Retain only the minimum
non-content anti-replay identity needed to prevent redispatch: immutable
identifiers, final disposition, terminal commit identity, and time.

Detailed operational traces have bounded retention only for active verification,
qualification, soak, or incident diagnosis. Purge them when that purpose ends.
Out-of-band backups provide recovery.

Formal anchors:

