## 20. Blocked execution

If execution is blocked by missing or contradictory project membership,
dependency ordering, specificity, formal authority, GO, test, or claim state,
ask the owner immediately.

State the exact missing condition and the smallest owner decision required.

Project authorization is not in this list. Agents do not check it, and dispatch
is complete proof, per section 3.

Authority: `GOV-PROJECT-IMPLEMENTATION-AUTHORIZATION-001`.

## 21. Missing or undiscoverable tools

If required tools, steps, or knowledge are difficult to find, treat that as a
GT-KB platform defect.

Do not improvise a second authority surface. Use safe read-only investigation,
identify the missing route, and capture a corrective work item or ADVISORY
according to section 14.

Formal-closure notice: no active formal artifact classifies undiscoverable
tooling as a platform defect. Closure gap C-5.

## 22. Known open platform defects

These are pointers, not authority. Re-query each work item before relying on it.

Live on 2026-08-31:

- `WI-7615` P0, open: `gt bridge file-implementation-proposal` denies a lawful
  filing through the retired `project-authorization-operation-time-enforcement`
  evaluator, reporting `no_current_covering_authorization` for a
  properly-membered work item. The compliance-gate half was corrected
  2026-08-31 by owner edit plus projection; the CLI half remains. Workaround:
  deposit a complete validated file under `bridge/` per section 19 and disclose
  the substitution. The same path's identity diagnostic cites
  `ADR-ELIMINATE-DURABLE-ROLE-ASSIGNMENT-001` and
  `DCL-NO-DURABLE-ROLE-IN-REGISTRY-001`, neither of which exists in MemBase.
  Also records 47 active `project_authorizations` rows with corrupted
  `scope_summary` text.
- `WI-7525` P0, open: the bridge status vocabulary has no single source.
  Approximately 13 status regexes, enums, and frozensets across `scripts/` and
  the package source, no two agreeing. No write-side set accepts
  `VERDICT-REJECTED`, and none accepts `NOT-READY`. Expect the writer to refuse
  the current vocabulary; disclose the substitution per section 6 rather than
  silently downgrading.
- `WI-6992` open: the emergency-bootstrap operational-event protocol is
  unimplemented, so section 9 cannot record its required evidence.
- `WI-7556` P1, open: `write_verdict.py` can crash with `UnicodeEncodeError`
  under the Windows cp1252 codepage. Disclosed workaround:
  `PYTHONIOENCODING=utf-8`.
- `WI-7557` P2, open: `gt bridge state-report` reports contradictory actionable
  state. Observed 2026-08-31: one render returned `NEW | 1` and
  `LO_ACTIONABLE_… | 0: (none)` simultaneously, and it still exposes obsolete
  `NO_ACTION` vocabulary. Re-read the exact operative thread before acting.
- `WI-7616` P2, open: `gt backlog list-phases` returns 2,036 rows for 16 phases;
  multi-line option values through the `gt` shim truncate the argv and report a
  misleading missing-option cause.
- `WI-7617` P3, open: the bridge WI-ID collision gate flags every cited related
  work item as a collision, penalizing the cross-referencing the applicability
  declaration requires.
- ADVISORY `gtkb-advisory-write-verdict-placeholder-and-intake-authority-001`:
  investigate the exact-init mutation-binding defect and verdict-placeholder
  behavior through current canonical state before relying on any workaround.

Residual references to TAFE, `NO-ACTION`, bridge-status `DEFERRED`,
`config/agent-control`, `.gtkb-state`, PAUTH-shaped authorization records, or
generated projections as authority are defects to correct.

## 23. Disorientation self-check

Stop, re-query canonical state, and ask the owner if you notice that you are:

- acting without an explicit role or activity;
- treating a projection, bridge file, scratchpad, handoff, registry row, or this
  guidance as durable authority;
- authoring a status your role may not author;
- repairing or inventing another author's routing header;
- citing TAFE, `NO-ACTION`, bridge-status `DEFERRED`, `config/agent-control`,
  `.gtkb-state`, or a PAUTH-shaped record as current authority;
- committing bridge material or generated projections;
- editing a generated projection;
- relying on a cached queue instead of current thread state;
- continuing an irreparable or unresolved chain;
- fragmenting work because of predicted context exhaustion;
- treating a deliberation as approval;
- treating a compatibility field or a PAUTH-shaped record as authorization;
- inferring an owner decision;
- improvising an authority surface because a capability is missing; or
- inventing a tool because the supported route is hard to find.

## 24. Absolute prohibitions

- Never directly edit a generated projection.
- Never write `.gtkb-state`.
- Never use `harness-state` without an explicit one-time owner exception.
- Never create an ad hoc project for an unmatched defect.
- Never commit bridge material or generated projections.
- Never cite bridge content from an authoritative artifact.
- Never reference scratchpad paths in bridge items.
- Never emit `GO`, ordinary `NO-GO`, `NOT-READY`, or `VERIFIED` as Prime
  Builder.
- Never formally review or commit your own context's work.
- Never perform the operational mutation you will independently verify.
- Never fabricate `WITHDRAWN`.
- Never inherit authority from an abandoned or discarded chain.
- Never reuse a verdict across threads.
- Never persist this guidance, a handoff, or mutable session state as an
  authority object.
- Never rewrite ordinary or formal history; supersede, retire, or perform new
  forward work.
- Never proceed on a guessed header, inferred activity, compatibility field,
  deliberation, or inferred owner approval.
- Never treat a missing capability as permission to improvise one.

